; the chemistry of wood as a construction material.
; 
; COMPOSITION:
; cellulose:    40-50%. (C6H10O5)n linear polymer. provides tensile strength.
; microfibrils wound helically — like steel rope structure.
; hemicellulose: 20-35%. branched polysaccharides. matrix between cellulose.
; lignin:       20-30%. complex aromatic polymer. binds fibres. waterproofs.
; gives wood its brown colour. very resistant to biodegradation.
; water:        20-50% (green wood). 10-15% (seasoned). affects all properties.
; 
; MOISTURE:
; wood is hygroscopic — absorbs water from air.
; cell walls swell when wet, shrink when dry → warping, cracking.
; fibre saturation point ~30% moisture: below this, strength increases as it dries.
; equilibrium moisture content: wood equilibrates with ambient humidity.
; kiln drying: removes moisture to 8-12% for stable structural timber.
; 
; COMBUSTION:
; pyrolysis begins at 200°C — cellulose breaks down to volatiles + char.
; ignition at ~300°C — volatiles ignite.
; char (carbon residue) insulates remaining wood — timber burns slowly.
; a 300mm timber beam retains structural integrity much longer than steel
; (which loses strength rapidly at 500°C). fire rating of timber is predictable.
; 
; BIODEGRADATION:
; fungi (white rot, brown rot) secrete cellulase and lignase enzymes.
; white rot: attacks lignin + cellulose → white stringy residue.
; brown rot: attacks cellulose only → brown cuboid pieces. rapid structural loss.
; prevention: keep dry (below 20% moisture), use preservatives (copper, boron),
; naturally durable species (teak, cedar, oak — high lignin/extractives).

